CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ size is 1 after one push_back: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ stored element value is exact: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^.*is unknown.*$
^CONVERSION ERROR$
^.*no body for callee.*$
--
[temp.inst]/1-2 with [dcl.typedef]: a member typedef declares the member and
forms the aliased type but does not require the aliased template's definition to
be instantiated; a typedef-name may denote an incomplete type.

Populated std::vector<int>: push_back -> _M_realloc_insert computes its offset
via __gnu_cxx::operator-(__normal_iterator, __normal_iterator), whose body needs
the iterator instance's member functions (base(), ...).  While the iterator
instance is completed during vector elaboration, the C++20 member alias
`using iterator_concept = std::__detail::__iter_concept<_Iterator>;` (a
merged_type alias-template result, not a bare cpp_name) cannot be elaborated
because the iterator-concept machinery reaches back through the still-incomplete
iterator.  typecheck_compound_body now keeps such a member alias lazy on failure
instead of abandoning the rest of the class body, so the sibling member
functions are instantiated and operator-'s body resolves base().  Before the
fix the methods were dropped, operator-'s body was emptied to a nondet stub, and
v.size() after push_back was nondeterministic (the assertions failed / were
vacuous).  The forbidden patterns above (`is unknown`, `CONVERSION ERROR`,
`no body for callee`) guard against the regression and against a vacuous pass.
